NAME
    head - print the leading part of files

SYNOPSIS
    head [-c COUNT] [-n COUNT] [-qv] [FILE...]
    head -COUNT [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the first 10 lines of each FILE. With no file, reads standard input.
    A FILE of - also selects standard input. At an interactive prompt that
    reads typed lines until Ctrl-D; see man sh.

    -c COUNT  Print the first COUNT bytes instead of lines.
    -n COUNT  Print the first COUNT lines. -COUNT is the same request.
    -q        Never print the ==> FILE <== header.
    -v        Always print the header, even for a single operand.

    Headers appear when more than one operand is named, separated by a blank
    line. Reading stops as soon as the requested count is met, so a bounded
    prefix of a large file costs only the bytes it prints.

    Every operand is attempted; a failure is reported and the remaining
    operands still run. A missing file exits 3, other failures exit 1.
